# Called Home - a bell tower, a ringer who has died, and a method nobody else can call.
# Written for the library. Opens inside a conversation, because 26 of the 39 stories before it open by inventorying
# something and then drawing an inference from it. Uses state mid-story, not only at the ending.

title: Called Home
start: start

== start ==
bg hall_corner
music room_tone
"Treble's going," says Margaret, and the treble goes, and then the two, and by the time it gets to you
the whole band is in it and you are pulling the fourth and counting under your breath the way you have
counted for thirty-one years.
"Stand," she says. The bells come down one at a time and stop in the order they were rung, which is
tidy, and nobody says anything for a moment, because the reason you are all here is in the notice
book on the table and everybody has read it.
-> the_notice

== the_notice ==
Geoff died on the Tuesday. He was seventy-nine and he had rung in this tower for fifty-four years
and he was the one who taught four of the seven people in this room, including you, and the notice
says his funeral is a fortnight on Thursday at the crematorium and that the family have asked
whether the tower would ring.
Which is not the question. The question is in the next line, in Margaret's handwriting, and it is
whether anybody in this band can CALL A COURSE OF PLAIN BOB MAJOR, half-muffled, at a funeral, in
front of a family, without it falling apart after two leads.
-> the_silence

== the_silence ==
Nobody answers. Margaret has called it, she called it for the last two funerals, and she is
seventy-six and she has been saying since the spring that she is not calling another one.
You have rung in this tower since you were fourteen and you have never called anything in your life.
You know the method. Knowing the method is not calling it: calling it means saying the words out
loud, on the day, into a room of people who are listening, and being right.
* Say you will call it. -> say_yes
* Say nothing, and hope somebody else does. -> say_nothing
* Ask Margaret to call this one and let you call the next one, whenever that is. -> ask_margaret

== say_yes ==
you: I'll call it.
Margaret looks at you for a moment and then does the thing she does, which is to write it in the
notice book without commenting, and the room relaxes by one degree because it is now somebody's job.
~ calling 1
-> the_practice

== say_nothing ==
The silence goes on long enough to become a thing that has been decided, and then the man on the
treble - who is forty-one and has called two peals in his life, both of them at another tower -
says he will have a go, and Margaret does not write it down straight away, which is the closest she
comes to an opinion.
He will be all right. He will probably be all right. You spend the whole of the next fortnight
knowing exactly what he is doing on the Wednesday evenings and not going.
-> the_practice

== ask_margaret ==
you: You call this one. I'll call the next.
her: There isn't a next, as far as this tower is concerned.
you: Then I'll call the one after.
She looks at you for a long moment and then agrees, and it is a real agreement rather than a
kindness, and she writes her own name in the book and under it she writes yours, for the next one,
with a question mark, which is her way of saying that she has heard you.
-> the_practice

== the_practice ==
The fortnight is four Wednesday evenings and a Saturday morning, and the band practises the funeral
touch eleven times, and on the last Wednesday it goes round twice without a fault, which has never
happened with this band in this tower.
if calling >= 1 -> you_called
-> the_man_called

== the_man_called ==
The man on the treble calls it on the Saturday as well, and gets it round, and afterwards he asks
you the question you have been dreading since the first Wednesday.
you: What.
man: You know it better than I do. You're counting under your breath.
-> the_asked

== you_called ==
They ring it for you and you hear it, and it is a different thing entirely from knowing it: the band
follows your hand and your voice and the method moves underneath everybody at once, and once it is
going it is not a thing you are doing, it is a thing you are in the middle of.
Margaret says one sentence at the end of the last Wednesday, which is the only comment she makes
that fortnight.
her: You'll do.
-> the_asked

== the_asked ==
Which leaves the last decision and it is not the one anybody would guess: whether to call it
HALF-MUFFLED or to ring it open.
Half-muffled is the funeral sound - one side of the clapper muffled so the bell gives a note and
then a dull echo - and it is what the family have asked for and what the book says.
Open is how Geoff rang a wedding, and how he rang the last peal he ever rang, and it is what he
would have wanted and nobody has said so out loud because it is not in the book.
* Half-muffled, because it is what the family asked for. -> muffled
* Open, because it is what he would have wanted and somebody has to say so. -> open
* Ask the family, on the Thursday, at the crematorium. -> ask_them

== muffled ==
You muffle the clappers on the Thursday morning with the leathers and the cable ties and the
sandbag under the sixth, which is the bell that rings the tenor down, and it takes forty minutes
and you do it the way Geoff showed you, which is the slow way, and which is why it is right.
The funeral is at two. The band is in the ringing chamber at half past one, which is earlier than
they have ever been for anything, and everybody is in a tie.
-> the_ending

== open ==
You put it to Margaret in the notice book rather than out loud, because a thing written is a thing
somebody can disagree with before the day, and she writes one word under it, which is FINE, and
then underneath that, in smaller writing, WHICH IS NOT THE SAME AS YES.
~ open_ringing 1
You ring it open on the Thursday and it is not a funeral sound at all, and the family are on the
other side of a wall, and it goes round eleven times, which is more than the book says and more
than the crematorium wanted, and Margaret does not stop you.
-> the_ending

== ask_them ==
You are at the crematorium at half past one and you do the thing that no ringer does, which is to
find the daughter of the dead man in a corridor and ask her a question about the bells.
She listens to the whole of it, the muffled and the open, and asks you one question back, which is
which one he would have picked, and you tell her, and she says do that then, and goes in.
~ open_ringing 1
-> the_ending

== the_ending ==
if open_ringing >= 1 -> ending_open
if calling >= 1 -> ending_called
-> ending_quiet

== ending_called ==
It goes round. That is all that happens and it is everything: eleven leads, half-muffled, called
out loud into a room of seven people who are following it, and it does not come apart, and at the
end the bells are left up in the order they were rung in.
Margaret writes two words in the notice book on the Friday, which are TOOK IT, and that is the
whole of what she says about the fortnight, and it is more than she has said about anything since
the spring, and the next funeral is sixteen months away and you will call that one too.
-> END

== ending_open ==
The bells go up and stay up through the silence and then they are rung DOWN in rounds, open, in the
order every tower rings down in, and by the time the tenor goes the family are coming out and the
sound is going across the car park in the flat four o'clock light.
It is not what was asked for and it is not what the book says, and the daughter stops on the step
and stands there for the whole of the ringing down, and then says one sentence to nobody in
particular, which is that he would have liked that, and goes to her car.
-> END

== ending_quiet ==
Somebody else called it and it went round, and you rang the fourth in a touch you did not call in
a tower where you have rung for thirty-one years, and nobody in the room knew the difference
except Margaret, who did, and who wrote nothing in the book at all that fortnight.
You go back on the following Wednesday. So does everybody. The bells go up and come down and the
notice book has a new page in it for the next thing, which is a wedding in the May, and the fourth
is your bell and it will be your bell for as long as you climb the stairs, and there is a version
of a life in a tower where that is enough, and you have not decided yet whether it is this one.
-> END
